WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
-- Hey Princess.
-- Hi Lindsay.

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.840
So... have you ever... done the fanfiction before?

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:06.940
Yes, many moons ago.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.860
Oh yeah? What was the first fandom you ever wrote for?

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:16.100
Avatar: The Last Airbender. Zutara lives on forever. What about you?

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.320
Phantom. [LAUGHTER] Phantom of the Opera, not the Billy Zane Phantom.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.440
The uh... the bad one. The problematic one.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:28.920
So on a scale from disappointed to disheartened, how deep is your shame?

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.860
Um... pretty deep, how about you?

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:38.120
You know. Sometimes I feel ashamed, but other times I'm like, "You know what? I like my trash."

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.680
And that is what we are: trash.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:52.560
Our relationship to fanfic as a concept has changed, but
not just fans' relationship to it - the professional

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.680
publishing world also has a wildly different relationship
to fanfic even from, like, ten years ago.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:03.440
So what has caused this shift, and what does that change even look like?

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.840
Part of the human fascination with narrative
is a desire to expand on the stories we hear.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.860
One popular narrative is the story of the
trickster hero, like in the American South

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:18.400
we have the Jack tale about a guy named Jack, or in the
black diaspora we have the spider-god character

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:24.039
Anansi - these characters have no original author, but
over the years have gained popularity

00:01:24.039 --> 00:01:26.420
in their communities through oral traditions.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:31.130
But the modern concept of copyright complicates
our relationship to characters and narratives,

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:35.600
which are no longer the intellectual property of a culture,
they’re the IP of a person

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.240
or a company.

00:01:37.249 --> 00:01:41.840
Fanfiction website Archive of Our Own, who I lost a Hugo
to last year, co-founder Francesa

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.170
Coppa defines it simply as “creative material featuring
characters [from] works whose copyright

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:47.170
is held by others.”

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:51.250
There’s kind of an unspoken understanding that you
aren’t supposed to make money off

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:56.040
of fanfiction, which is part of why copyright
holders usually let it slide.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:01.771
And as a result it exists in a legal grey
area - most IP owners let it exist, and some

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:05.100
even encourage it because it facilitates a
good relationship with fans.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.460
But for me, what’s really interesting about
fan fiction is the negative connotations that

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:14.940
have dogged it for the last few decades; that fan fiction
is embarrassing, that it's neither

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.409
real writing, nor a good writing exercise,
and that it is something to be relegated to the

00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:21.180
daydreams of pre-teen girls.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:25.280
But not only that, professional authors'
relationship to fanfiction has changed.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.960
Many authors began as fanfiction writers, and are kind
of out and proud about that now.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:33.740
Some continue to write it for fun, and sometimes they just

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:39.340
swap out the names of the copyrighted characters and
publish their fanfic as original fiction.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:44.060
Where fanfiction used to be seen as a joke, writers today
have been “coming out of the closet"

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:49.100
so to speak about their own history with fanfiction and
how it helped form their professional careers.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:54.170
That’s right, we have Hugo and Nebula winning
fanfiction writers now.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:55.420
*air horn*

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:56.420
Some history -

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:01.269
What we know as fanfiction has its roots not
on the internet but in fanzines, specifically

00:03:01.269 --> 00:03:05.749
science fiction fanzines from the early 20th
century, which actually published fanfiction

00:03:05.749 --> 00:03:06.749
at the time.

00:03:06.749 --> 00:03:11.040
To Harvard English professor and writer Dr.
Stephanie Burt, fan fiction is a phenomena

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.120
that has always existed in some form or another
as a way for fans to meaningfully interact

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:16.940
with media they like. Says Burt:

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:20.830
“First there were fans of science-fiction
novels and magazines who held conventions

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.150
and traded self-published journals as early
as the nineteen-thirties.

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:28.610
Or: first there was Sherlock Holmes, whose
devotees, hooked by serial publication, pushed

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:31.860
for more stories, formed clubs, and wrote
their own.”

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:37.580
Some say that fan fiction itself isn’t writing--that
real writers don’t participate in it.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:39.940
That, if you want to write, you have to write
original stories.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:45.200
You know, James Joyce and William Shakespeare
and Dante and Salman Rushdie.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:50.040
Says Burt again: “No clearer path from new
writers to potentially interested readers

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.450
has existed in the history of civilization….

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:55.370
not all stories are stories that other people
will seek out.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:04:00.220
If you can work your memories, hypotheses,
or fantasies about living away from home,

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:04.840
or about gender transition, or about retirement into a
story about Bruce Wayne and Dick Grayson, maybe

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:09.680
the many people who care about Batman and Robin will
care about your thoughts and experiences, too.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:14.860
This challenges the idea that fan fiction
is the toy of the creatively-bereft; that

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:20.800
the only good fiction is writing that is 100%
original--why, for example, is John Milton’s

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:26.000
Paradise Lost or James Joyce’s Ulysses 'canon' without
ever questioning their originality, even

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.660
though they are retellings of the Bible and
The Odyssey, respectively?

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:35.130
Well, part of it is copyright jankiness - Ulysses
and Paradise Lost are based on stories that

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:39.440
were never copyrighted, because copyright
was not a thing in Homer’s time.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.120
We have a wholly different relationship to
copyright than James Joyce and John Milton did.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.940
But that can’t be all of it.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:52.180
Ever since modern fanfiction gained in fanzines,
particularly Star Trek fanzines, it’s predominantly

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:54.220
been the wheelhouse of women.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:58.140
Best-selling author and Hugo winner Seanen
McGuire sees the phenomenon as partially a

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:02.560
response to a lack of female-oriented storytelling
in mainstream and published mediums.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.480
In her essay “The Bodies of the Girls Who Made Me:
Fan Fiction and the Modern World”, she writes:

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:11.380
“Everyone who grows up on a diet of Western
media learns, on some level, to accept The

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:15.440
Default as their avatar, because we historically
haven’t had much choice.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.220
Want to be the hero, instead of the love interest,
the scrappy sidekick, or the villain?

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:21.520
Embrace The Default.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.300
Learn to have empathy with The Default.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.110
He’s what you get.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:28.750
[...] We stopped writing about ourselves and
started writing avatars, characters who could

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:31.750
represent us in the stories without quite
being us.”

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:36.290
It’s no coincidence that a large part of
the fan fiction community has been built by

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:43.170
women, POC and LGBTQ writers in a professional
publishing world that has only relatively recently

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:44.790
begun to represent them.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:49.690
Fanfiction is having something of a reckoning
over the last few years: writers like Seanan

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:54.900
McGuire or NK Jemisin, successful authors
who are vocal proponents of what fan fiction

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.520
has meant to them as creative minds,

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:02.160
or websites like Archive of our Own creating living
documentation of fan fiction’s vast

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:07.540
community while advocating free speech, or academics
like Francesca Coppa publishing

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:10.980
both commercial fan fiction and research on
the subject.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:15.000
Star Trek fandom was the starting point for
many science fiction professional writers.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.640
Jacqueline Lichtenberg was an early Star Trek
fanfic writer.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:22.900
She went on to publish her own fantasy series,
starting in 1974, and Lois McMaster Bujold,

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:26.660
one of the most prolific and beloved science fiction
writers still writing today and writer of

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:30.300
the Vorkosigan saga also dabbled in Trek fanworks early in her career.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:34.030
But these are early examples of professional
authors getting their start in fanworks - which

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:39.720
is NOT the same thing as repackaging your
fanfiction as original fiction and then publishing it.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:44.760
When fictional fanworks are re-written and
sold as profic, the fannish code for this

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:47.220
is “filing off the serial numbers.”

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:51.910
50 Shades of Grey — which originated as
Twilight fanfic — might be the cliche of

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:56.020
fanfiction reworked for publication, but it’s
actually been a thing for a while.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:01.911
There are a few obscure incidences from the
olden times - for instance, in 1985 author

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:08.590
Teri White published a Starsky and Hutch fanfic
as "Cowboy Blues,” and in 1997 author Susan

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:14.000
Matthews reworked her Star Wars fanfics into "An
Exchange of Hostages" and the Jurisdiction series.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.760
You love to see it.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:20.100
But since the 2000’s, perhaps in no small
part because publishers have taken note of

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:26.100
fanfic authors who already have big followings of their
own, it’s gone from anomaly to actual trend.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:30.760
Cassandra Claire was a Harry Potter and Lord of the
Rings BNF, which for those not in fandom

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:33.120
parlance know means “Big Name Fan."

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.760
She became hugely popular in Harry Potter
fandom for her Draco Trilogy, much of which

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:40.460
was reworked into her bestselling Mortal Instruments series.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:46.840
And a shocking amount of one fanfic subgenre called
RPF – which means 'real person fic', which is exactly

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:51.280
what it sounds like – has been reworked for publication... probably because you can’t really copyright

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:55.260
a person, so there’s a lot less copyright jank there.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:08:00.120
You can just write about a real person and...
pretend it's a fictional character.

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:05.400
After is the most famous example, reworked
from the fanfic of the same name about a college

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:12.440
alternate univere, or AU, featuring Harry
Styles from One Direction as a... bad Harry Styles,

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:15.040
but there is a truly staggering amount of One Direction fanfic

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:18.200
that has had its serial numbers filed off for publication.

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:23.200
So not only are some authors permissive of
fanfiction, even starting their careers as

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:27.580
fanfic writers before making the transition,
some just cut out the middle man and publish

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:30.650
their slightly reworked fanfiction as original
fiction.

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:33.680
JK Rowling has been generally supportive of fanfiction,

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:37.959
but not all authors have such a benign relationship to the concept of fanfiction.

00:08:37.959 --> 00:08:42.700
Authors who aren’t so keen on fanfic range
from the mildly disapproving, like uncle

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:48.370
George RR Martin, who has said "My characters
are my children…I don’t want people making

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:50.140
off with them, thank you.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:52.160
Even people who say they love my children.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:56.680
I’m sure that’s true, I don’t doubt
the sincerity of the affection, but still.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:59.600
No one gets to abuse the people of Westeros but me.”

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:01.980
And the writers of Game of Thrones.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:07.160
And that brings us to vampire chronicles author Anne
Rice, who was once very hardcore with the whole

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:12.100
cease and desist letter thing, to the point where pretty
much the entirety of vampire chronicles fanfiction

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:16.800
throughout the 90’s and 2000’s existed
on locked down livejournal groups, with some

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.180
fans even claiming to have been harassed by
Rice’s lawyers.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:22.000
The characters are copyrighted.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.300
It upsets me terribly to even think about
fan fiction with my characters.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:29.200
I advise my readers to write your own original stories with your own characters.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:32.200
It is absolutely essential that you respect
my wishes.”

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:35.400
That’s my Anne rice voice.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:39.100
Although, to her credit, she has mellowed
out a little since then.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:43.200
Even pro authors like Seanan McGuire, who
continue to write fanfic themselves, would

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:47.920
prefer that you don’t send your own to them, 
because it can muddy the waters of whose

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.200
idea is whose.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:55.420
Said McGuire in a tweet from 2017 - “Fanfic
is awesome and amazing and I love it and if

00:09:55.420 --> 00:10:00.140
you try to tell me about your fanfic, I will
shut you down like a Blockbuster Video.”

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:06.000
Some authors have cited a 1992 incident involving
author Marion Zimmer - she was initially very

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.460
supportive of fandom and fanfiction in particular.

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:12.660
But while writing a new novel, she realized
that it touched on themes of a fanfiction

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:14.000
she had already read.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:16.400
Bradley decided to scrap the novel altogether rather than

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:17.780
risk a lawsuit.

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:22.360
So where fanfic used to exist mostly in the
underground in the realm of pure hobbyism

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:26.200
and sometimes in locked LiveJournal communities
out of fear of litigious authors,

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:30.380
fanfiction's emergence into the mainstream has changed our relationship to it.

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:35.000
Professional authors are fine admitting their past,
and sometimes present, as fanfiction

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:40.300
writers. It’s no longer this shameful activity done in
shadow, but an activity that is recognized as

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:43.100
a valid way to improve your skills as a
writer.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:45.100
As Neil Gaiman puts it…

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:48.400
“I think that all writing is useful for
honing writing skills.

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:53.300
I think you get better as a writer by writing,
and whether that means that you’re writing

00:10:53.300 --> 00:11:00.100
a singularly deep and moving novel about the pain or pleasure of modern existence or you’re writing a

00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:06.900
Smeagol-Gollum [slashfic], you’re still putting one damn word after another and learning as a writer."

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:10.200
And that is my best Neil Gaiman impression.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:13.960
How did you know about my Smeagol-Gollum slashfic,
Neil Gaiman??

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:17.200
Cease and desist!

